Battuta {{ tag }} install instructions: Oracle Ampere (main machine)
==================================================================

Bundle:        main (CoS, Manager, general workers, memory, artifacts, speech)
Platform:      Oracle Linux 9 on AArch64 (OCI Ampere A1)
Tool version:  {{ tool_version }}
Release page:  {{ release_url }}
Repository:    {{ repo_url }}

Run these steps as the ordinary bot user. Do not use sudo. Stop on any failed
command. Follow RELEASE.md in the archive for the detailed setup; it is the
authoritative guide for the setup steps in sections 4 to 6 below.

1. Download
-----------

Download into a new, empty directory outside the installation path.

  Archive:            {{ release_url }}/{{ archive_name }}
  Archive checksum:   {{ release_url }}/{{ archive_name }}.sha256
  Archive SHA-256:    {{ archive_sha256 }}
  Wheel checksums:    {{ release_url }}/python-tools-SHA256SUMS
  Tool version file:  {{ release_url }}/python-tools-version.txt
{% for wheel in wheels %}
  {{ wheel.package }}:
    {{ wheel.url }}
    SHA-256: {{ wheel.sha256 }}
{% endfor %}

2. Verify checksums
-------------------

Every check must report OK. Never extract or install an unverified download.

  version={{ tag }}
  platform={{ platform }}
  archive="{{ archive_name }}"
  sha256sum -c "$archive.sha256"
  sha256sum -c python-tools-SHA256SUMS
  tool_version="$(cat python-tools-version.txt)"

3. Install or upgrade the Python tools
--------------------------------------

Install the wheels for this bundle. Each wheel needs its own tool
installation so its command is on PATH. `--force` replaces an earlier version
of the same tool, which is how upgrades are applied.

{% for wheel in wheels %}
  uv tool install --managed-python --python 3.12 --no-build --force "./{{ wheel.filename }}"
{% endfor %}
  export PATH="$(uv tool dir --bin):$PATH"
  battuta-project --help >/dev/null
  battuta-artifact --help >/dev/null
  battuta-memory --help >/dev/null
  battuta-speech --help >/dev/null
  battuta-doc --help >/dev/null
  battuta-bot-setup --help >/dev/null
  battuta-packaging --help >/dev/null

4. Extract the release and read RELEASE.md
------------------------------------------

Unpack the archive to read the guide; the installation is extracted separately.

  mkdir release && tar -xzf "$archive" -C release
  less release/RELEASE.md

Ask the owner for the absolute installation path to use. It must be new and empty,
and must not contain spaces. Never reuse a path, and never clear ~/.battuta or service
data. If the owner has not given a path, stop and ask rather than choosing one.

  install_path="<absolute path the owner approved>"
  deployment={{ deployment }}

Then follow RELEASE.md from "Choose the installation bundle" through step 6,
using the same archive and `install_path`. Sections 1 to 3 above cover RELEASE.md
steps 1 and 2, so start at RELEASE.md step 3 with `battuta-packaging extract-bots`.

5. Upgrade
----------

Install a new release into a new, empty path that the owner approves.
Stop affected services and get owner approval before switching the running
installation. Existing `~/.battuta` configuration is kept.

6. Launch the server
--------------------

Launch is an owner decision. After setup validation passes, generate the
services and review the printed activation command, which covers memory,
artifacts, speech, CoS, Manager, and the selected workers. Activate it only with
owner approval.

  battuta-packaging install-services --root "$install_path" \
    --env-file "$HOME/.battuta/.env.prod" --node "$node" --deployment "$deployment"

7. Links
--------

  Release page:      {{ release_url }}
  Repository:        {{ repo_url }}
  Archive (contains RELEASE.md):  {{ release_url }}/{{ archive_name }}
